Z||000000||shiloh187||04-26-2001||01:08 AM||||I am looking for a step by step way to turn a coup into a GT, while keeping the coupe sail panels and rear end (nothcback)... isearched for it but could not seem to find it... i need to know what to replace, etc..<P>shiloh||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||04-26-2001||02:53 AM||||you mean you want to swap to the aero nose? (if you keep the back end and the sail panels, it won't be a gt except for the front end.. then again, a coupe could never be a gt anyway.. no matter how many panels were swapped..) to do this you need the GT nose, the crash stuff behind it (can't recall the name.. ben studying all night, brain is full of biotechnology terms..) and also the wheel well liners, and the trim pieces. (ribbed, rounded, etc)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000002||shiloh187||04-26-2001||01:59 PM||||well you sort of hit it on what i want to do... i want to make a mutt of sorts... coupe back end with gt front end, and gt doors, etc ... i really like the dual-tone painted way a gt looks.. but i love the back on the coupe notchbacks...  so basically can i get all the panels from a GT and put them onto the frame of my coupe?<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||04-26-2001||02:10 PM||||The 85GT and 86SE are what you are describing.  Aero nose with ground effects and notchback rear.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MontanaFiero||04-26-2001||02:31 PM||||Basically what Shilo wants is like an '85 GT, from what I gather. <BR>You will need all of the aero trim plus the aero rocker panels, and of course the aero nose. Consider which molding you have, because grooved molding gets an SE nose (or 85 GT), round molding gets GT nose.<BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hartz||04-26-2001||03:37 PM||||I'm in the process of doing basically the same thing.  I have 99% of all the parts I need - Or thought I did.  Why do you need the inner fender liners?  Do the coupe fender liners not match up?  Are they really different?  Can the coupe liners be modified to fit?  Trim?  Drill?  Any thoughts appreciated.<P>Thanks,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000006||shiloh187||04-26-2001||07:22 PM||||i guess that is where i am asking for help.. the aero trim and rocker panels.  i am not sure what they all consist of.  can i just grab them off any gt model?  i found a gt model in a salvage yard here, that i could get the body panels off of, if i know what to take, still in search of a nose though.<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000007||shiloh187||04-26-2001||07:42 PM||||i found a picture of basically what it is i want to do..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/gallery/Cars/DSC00001_3.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/gallery/Cars/DSC00001_3.jpg</A> <P>but would not paint it that color and would add side scoops (someone had nice ones for $100 a pair??) and a spoiler.. and of course aftermarket rims..<P>a nice v6 wouldnt hurt either but lets not get carried away yet..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||04-26-2001||07:45 PM||||It,s easier to replace the fenders, outer door skin, and quarter panel if available since the upper ground effects are attached through the skin. Guess you could drill them but you would need the originals to get corect mesurments for the oblong holes.<BR> also lower rocker panel  (you also need the metal support under it) and the front and rear facias and there egg crate shock asorbers. The aero nose uses a modified inner fender and could be added to but if you are getting the nose they are usually availiable.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mbramble||04-26-2001||07:58 PM||||The wheel well liners are shaped differently around the front (or rear) outer edges to conform to the shape of either the coupe or aero fascias.  The fascias are attached to the wheel well liners.  I saw a set in the junkyard where someone had added on pieces to a coupe's front wheel well liners to fit the shape of the aero fascia that had obviously been added.  They had stapled the pieces (cut from ??) to the coupe liners.  Looked pretty crappy...<P>To do the complete aero conversion you would need:<BR>- front and rear fascias<BR>- front and rear "egg crates (under the fascias)<BR>- front and rear side markers and fender and door moulding pieces to match the molding on the rear fascia<BR>- wheel well liners<BR>- aero rocker panels and underlying metal support<BR>- front and rear fenders* <BR>- outer door panels* <BR>* with the ground effects attached  <P>If you just get the ground effects pieces (and their mounting hardware) you will have to drill the standard coupe panels to mount the ground effects pieces to them.  I don't think it would be too fun to try to drill the holes so that the ground effects pieces mount correctly.<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 04-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wolfhound||04-26-2001||08:17 PM||||mbramble, The piece you saw added to the front inner fender is the way the factory did it. thats stock.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mbramble||04-26-2001||08:31 PM||||No, no,  this one wasn't stock!!  Unless the guy who put these together at the factory was drunk!!  It looked almost like the guy used tarpaper!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||shiloh187||04-26-2001||08:32 PM||||well maybe i can get edsb52 to come with me to get the pieces, &lt;wink&gt;.... will the GT rear fascia fit on while keeping the notchback lighting and decklid?<P>shiloH||||reg||1
Z||000013||mbramble||04-26-2001||09:07 PM||||Yes -- you are basically putting together a car in the style of the Indy, 85GT and 86/87SE -- an aero notchback....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Wolfhound||04-26-2001||09:21 PM||||mbramble, never the less, the factory method is to staple on an additional piece to the coupe inner fender. <BR> I did this identical swap about a year ago and something I noticed is the 86 GT rear facia is slightly longer (front to rear than the)85GT and 86SE that has the embossed Pontiac. The difference is in the rear most part of the facia. It doesn't effect fit.<BR>Just somthing I noticed in the process.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mbramble||04-26-2001||09:49 PM||||Yes, they do use staples but not like those out of a regular staple gun!!  These things looked pitiful.  Of course the rest of the car looked the same.  The top had been cut off and some sort of fiberglass roof was latched on.  Nothing lined up properly, it looked hideous.  And there was a huge wood/fiberglass piece on the rear deck where some sort of "wing" had been ripped off.  And it was painted yellow --  looked like from a can of Krylon paint, with overspray everywhere.  Dang it was ugly!!  But it still had a few items in it that I could use....<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 04-26-2001).]||||reg||1
